---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/divorce-cost-uae/" lang="en" hreflang={hreflang} schema={articleSchema}>
  <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="overline">Cost Guide · UAE 2026</p>
      <h1>Divorce Cost in UAE 2026: Court Fees, Lawyer Fees &amp; DLD Property Transfer</h1>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suoninen, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated July 2026</p>
      <p class="hero-sub">The full picture of what divorce costs in the UAE: government court fees, lawyer rates, translation and attestation, expert and welfare reports, private mediation, and the Dubai Land Department fees for transferring a home. With worked examples and the Muslim versus non-Muslim and Dubai versus Abu Dhabi differences that change your bill.</p>
      <div class="hero-meta">
        <span>Updated: July 2026</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>Figures indicative; they vary by firm and case</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="main-content">
    <div class="container">

      <!-- Quick summary box -->
      <div class="summary-box">
        <h2>Quick Cost Summary: Divorce in UAE (Indicative)</h2>
        <div class="summary-grid">
          <div class="summary-card summary-card--highlight">
            <p class="summary-label">Uncontested Divorce</p>
            <p class="summary-amount">AED 12,000–30,000</p>
            <p class="summary-note">All-in (court fees + lawyer + translation). Both parties agree on every term.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="summary-card">
            <p class="summary-label">Contested Divorce</p>
            <p class="summary-amount">AED 45,000–90,000+</p>
            <p class="summary-note">Assets, alimony or property in dispute. 1 to 2 years.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="summary-card">
            <p class="summary-label">Custody / Complex</p>
            <p class="summary-amount">AED 80,000–150,000+</p>
            <p class="summary-note">Custody, business valuations, cross-border elements. Can run 2 to 3 years.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="summary-note-footer">All figures are indicative totals including court fees, lawyer fees and translation. They vary by firm and case. Property transfers add separate Dubai Land Department fees (see below).</p>
      </div>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How the Full UAE Divorce Bill Adds Up</h2>
        <p>Most cost guides quote one number, usually a lawyer fee, and stop there. Your real bill is a stack of six separate items, and any of them can be the one that surprises you. This guide combines all of them in one place: court filing fees, lawyer fees, certified translation and attestation, expert and welfare reports, private mediation, and Dubai Land Department (DLD) property-transfer fees. The property-transfer piece is the one almost nobody explains, and it can dwarf every other cost if a home changes hands at the wrong moment.</p>
        <p>Two things shape the total more than anything else. The first is how much you and your spouse actually agree on: an uncontested settlement can cost a fraction of a fought case. The second is which law and court apply to you. Muslim divorces run under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> (in force since 15 April 2025, replacing the old 2005 Personal Status Law) on the Sharia track. Non-Muslim residents divorce under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022</strong>, or in Abu Dhabi under <strong>Law No. 14 of 2021</strong>, on a no-fault civil track that is usually faster and cheaper. For the full legal picture, see our <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law overview</a> and the <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">complete divorce in UAE guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <img
        src="/images/uae-legal-documents.webp"
        alt="UAE legal documents with gold pen, divorce cost guide"
        width="900" height="507"
        loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        class="article-img"
      />

      <!-- Court fees table -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>UAE Court &amp; Ancillary Fees: What You Pay Beyond the Lawyer</h2>
        <p>Government court fees are set by each emirate's judiciary and are modest compared with legal fees. But the ancillary items around them, translation, attestation, expert reports and welfare reports, add up quickly in a disputed case. The table below covers the full set of court-side costs for a Dubai case; Abu Dhabi and Sharjah have similar structures with minor differences. All figures are indicative.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Fee Item</th>
                <th>Amount</th>
                <th>Notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {courtFees.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{row.item}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{row.cost}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Court-side costs typically total AED 2,000–5,000 for a straightforward uncontested case. Contested cases with multiple hearings, financial experts and welfare reports can reach AED 10,000–25,000 in court-side outlay before any lawyer fee. All figures indicative and vary by case.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Lawyer fees -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Lawyer Fees: The Biggest Cost Variable</h2>
        <p>Legal fees are where divorce costs diverge the most, driven by how much you and your spouse agree on. UAE lawyers typically charge a fixed fee for simple matters, per hearing, or a monthly retainer plus hourly time for long-running contested cases. For a full breakdown of how firms structure their pricing and how to compare quotes, see our dedicated guide to <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees in the UAE</a>.</p>

        <div class="lawyer-cards">
          {lawyerFees.map(fee => (
            <div class="lawyer-card">
              <div class="lawyer-card-header">
                <h3>{fee.type}</h3>
                <span class="lawyer-cost">{fee.range}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="lawyer-desc">{fee.desc}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Fixed vs. Per-Hearing vs. Retainer Billing</h3>
          <p>Most UAE divorce lawyers use one of three billing models:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fixed fee</strong>: common for uncontested cases (AED 8,000–25,000 all-in). Clear budget, no surprises.</li>
            <li><strong>Per hearing</strong>: AED 1,500–5,000 per court session. If the case runs long, costs accumulate fast.</li>
            <li><strong>Retainer plus hourly</strong>: common for complex contested cases. A monthly retainer plus hourly time.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Always ask for a written fee agreement before engaging any lawyer. Verbal estimates are not binding under UAE law.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="subhead">Indicative Hourly Rates</h3>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Seniority</th><th>Hourly rate (indicative)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {hourlyRates.map((r, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{r.level}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{r.rate}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Rates vary by firm, seniority and case complexity. Location matters too: compare our guides to <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-dubai/">divorce lawyers in Dubai</a> and <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-abu-dhabi/">divorce lawyers in Abu Dhabi</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- DLD property transfer -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Dubai Land Department Property-Transfer Fees</h2>
        <p>If you own a home in Dubai, the single largest number in your divorce may not be a lawyer or the court, it is the DLD transfer fee. And it turns entirely on timing. Transferring property between spouses <strong>while still legally married</strong> qualifies for the reduced gift (hiba) rate of just <strong>0.125% of the property value plus AED 530</strong>. Wait until the divorce is finalised and the ordinary transfer fee of <strong>4% plus AED 540</strong> applies instead. On a AED 2 million home that is the difference between roughly AED 3,030 and AED 80,540, about AED 77,000. Where both parties already agree on who keeps the property, completing the transfer before the decree is a rare and legitimate way to save a very large sum.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Fee Item</th>
                <th>Amount</th>
                <th>Notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {dldFees.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{row.item}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{row.cost}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Figures per the Dubai Land Department fee schedule. The DLD transfer itself is processed in about 25 to 30 minutes when the paperwork is complete. UAE law has no community-property concept, so property follows the title deed unless a contribution claim is filed. For how this works in practice, see <a href="/en/property-division-divorce-uae/">property division in UAE divorce</a> and <a href="/en/divorce-property-sale-uae/">selling property during a UAE divorce</a>.</p>

        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Timing Warning</h3>
          <p>The pre-divorce gift transfer only works if <strong>both spouses genuinely agree</strong> on who keeps the property and cooperate on the paperwork before the divorce is finalised. If ownership is disputed, do not attempt it: a transfer made to defeat the other party's claim can be challenged. Get legal advice on your specific case first.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Muslim vs non-Muslim -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Muslim vs. Non-Muslim: How the Cost Differs</h2>
        <p>The UAE now runs a dual family-law system, and which track you fall under changes both the process and the price.</p>
        <p><strong>Muslim divorce (Federal Decree-Law 41/2024).</strong> Cases run on the Sharia track, starting with mandatory Family Guidance and a 60-day reconciliation window (reduced from 90 under the old 2005 law). The reconciliation stage is low-cost in itself, but it adds time, and time is what drives lawyer fees. A wife-initiated <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">khulʿ</a> can be simpler and cheaper where the husband agrees, though the wife may have to return the mahr. Where custody or assets are fought, Muslim contested cases sit at the top of the cost range.</p>
        <p><strong>Non-Muslim divorce (Federal Decree-Law 41/2022).</strong> Either spouse can petition for a no-fault divorce without proving grounds, and mandatory reconciliation does not apply. Fewer procedural stages mean fewer hearings and lower fees for an uncontested split. There is no iddah waiting period and no guardian-approval requirement. Non-Muslim residents may also opt to have their home-country law applied if they raise it in the petition, though proving foreign law adds an expert cost.</p>

        <h3 class="subhead">Dubai vs. Abu Dhabi for Non-Muslims</h3>
        <p>Venue matters. The <strong>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</strong>, created under Abu Dhabi Law 14/2021, hears non-Muslim family cases in both Arabic and English with bilingual forms. It is widely regarded as the fastest and cheapest route for a non-Muslim uncontested divorce, an amicable no-fault case can complete in under a month. Dubai's onshore Personal Status Court applies the federal 41/2022 civil law but conducts proceedings in Arabic, so expats there carry more translation cost. Note that despite marketing claims, the DIFC and ADGM courts do <strong>not</strong> grant divorces; personal-status matters sit with the onshore family courts and the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court. To compare the filing routes, see <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file for divorce in Dubai</a> and <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Worked examples -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Worked Examples: What a Typical Divorce Actually Costs</h2>
        <p>Real bills are easier to picture with a full stack rather than a single fee. These indicative examples add up every component. Your case will differ.</p>

        <div class="example-card">
          <h3>Example 1: Uncontested civil divorce, no property (Abu Dhabi)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Court filing and first-instance fees: <strong>AED 2,000–3,000</strong></li>
            <li>Certified translation and attestation: <strong>AED 1,000–2,000</strong></li>
            <li>Lawyer (fixed-fee, both terms agreed): <strong>AED 8,000–15,000</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="example-total">All-in: roughly <strong>AED 11,000–20,000</strong>, completed in around a month via the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="example-card">
          <h3>Example 2: Uncontested Muslim divorce with a Dubai property transfer</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Court and Family Guidance fees: <strong>AED 2,500–4,000</strong></li>
            <li>Translation, attestation and notary: <strong>AED 1,500–3,000</strong></li>
            <li>Lawyer (fixed-fee settlement): <strong>AED 10,000–20,000</strong></li>
            <li>DLD pre-divorce gift transfer of an AED 2m flat (0.125% + AED 530 + trustee + title): <strong>AED 6,000–8,000</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="example-total">All-in: roughly <strong>AED 20,000–35,000</strong>. Had the same property transferred after the divorce at 4%, the DLD cost alone would jump by about AED 77,000.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="example-card">
          <h3>Example 3: Contested divorce with a custody dispute</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Contested court fees (multiple claims joined): <strong>AED 4,000–8,000</strong></li>
            <li>Translation and attestation of financial disclosure: <strong>AED 2,000–4,000</strong></li>
            <li>Welfare report plus financial expert: <strong>AED 6,000–15,000</strong></li>
            <li>Lawyer (retainer plus hearings over 18 to 24 months): <strong>AED 80,000–150,000+</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="example-total">All-in: <strong>AED 90,000–180,000+</strong>. Custody battles are the most expensive and slowest cases in the UAE system.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Whether your case is contested or uncontested is the biggest single lever on all of the above. See <a href="/en/contested-vs-uncontested-divorce-uae/">contested vs uncontested divorce in the UAE</a> and the <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/">UAE divorce timeline</a> for how duration maps to cost.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- What drives costs -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Drives Your Divorce Cost Higher</h2>

        <div class="driver-grid">
          {[
            { factor: 'Dispute over child custody', impact: 'Very High', desc: 'Custody cases involve social reports, psychologist assessments, multiple hearings, and often appeals. A contested custody case can run 18–36 months.' },
            { factor: 'Property and asset division', impact: 'High', desc: 'If matrimonial property, business interests, or UAE property are disputed, valuations, expert witnesses, and disclosure proceedings add significantly to costs.' },
            { factor: 'Spouse is abroad', impact: 'High', desc: 'Serving divorce papers internationally, obtaining foreign court documents, and managing timezone differences all add procedural costs and delays.' },
            { factor: 'Non-Muslim / foreign law cases', impact: 'Medium–High', desc: 'Applying home-country law requires the court to receive evidence about foreign law, which typically needs a qualified legal expert to testify or submit a report.' },
            { factor: 'Unresponsive spouse', impact: 'Medium', desc: 'If your spouse ignores proceedings, the case defaults after notice periods. This adds time but not necessarily huge cost if you have a lawyer managing it.' },
            { factor: 'Translation volume', impact: 'Medium', desc: 'Each foreign document requires certified translation and often attestation. Large financial disclosure cases (bank records, foreign property documents) accumulate significant translation costs.' },
          ].map(d => (
            <div class="driver-card">
              <div class="driver-header">
                <span class="driver-factor">{d.factor}</span>
                <span class={`driver-impact driver-impact--${d.impact.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z]/g, '-')}`}>{d.impact}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="driver-desc">{d.desc}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- How to reduce costs -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How to Reduce Your Divorce Costs in UAE</h2>

        <div class="tips-list">
